'en_content'=><<<'HTML'
<p>A dented can raises a very specific question: is the damage merely cosmetic, or could it have compromised the seal? The answer depends less on how unattractive the can looks and more on <strong>where the dent is, how deep it is and whether a seam has been affected</strong>. A shallow mark on the smooth body of an otherwise intact can is not the same as a sharp impact on the top or bottom seam.</p>
<p>Commercial canned food remains protected as long as the container stays hermetically sealed after processing. If a dent breaks, loosens or severely deforms a seam, the issue is no longer appearance: a route for contamination may have been created.</p>
<h2>What food-safety guidance says</h2>
<p>Food inspection guidance distinguishes a <strong>small dent</strong> in a can that is otherwise in good condition from a <strong>deep dent</strong>. Deep dents on top, bottom or side seams are particularly concerning because they can damage the closure.</p>
<p>Leaking, bulging, heavily rusted, punctured or badly damaged cans should also be discarded. Tasting a little food to see whether a suspicious can is “fine” is not an appropriate safety test.</p>
<h2>Why seams matter so much</h2>
<p>A can is not simply a metal cylinder. Its ends are attached by mechanically formed seams designed to maintain an airtight closure. The smooth body may tolerate a minor knock without losing integrity, while a sharp deformation directly on a seam can change the geometry of that closure.</p>
<p>This is why a broad shallow dent away from the edges can be less concerning than a smaller but sharp dent exactly where the lid joins the body.</p>
<h2>What counts as a deep dent?</h2>
<p>There is no perfect household rule based on millimetres. Think instead of a clearly pronounced hollow where the metal has been strongly pushed in, folded or formed into a sharp point. If your fingertip can sit deeply in the indentation or the impact has created a hard crease, it is no longer a trivial surface mark.</p>
<p>If that damage touches a seam, discarding the can is the sensible choice.</p>
<h2>Signs to discard a can before opening</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Bulging</strong> ends or body.</li>
<li><strong>Leakage</strong> or dried residue suggesting contents escaped.</li>
<li>A <strong>deep or sharp dent</strong>.</li>
<li>Strong impact on a <strong>top, bottom or side seam</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Heavy rust</strong> that may have weakened the metal.</li>
<li>A puncture, crack or visible opening.</li>
<li>Deformation severe enough to prevent normal stacking or opening.</li>
</ul>
<h2>What about a tiny dent on the side?</h2>
<p>A shallow, smooth indentation away from the seams, on a dry can with no swelling and no other damage, may simply be external mechanical damage. Even then, it makes sense to use that can before undamaged ones and inspect it again before opening.</p>
<p>If a retailer delivers a badly dented can, requesting a replacement is also reasonable. There is no benefit in accepting avoidable uncertainty in a newly purchased product.</p>
<h2>What should you check when opening it?</h2>
<p>A normal can may make a small pressure sound, but a container that <strong>spurts liquid or foam abnormally</strong>, has a distinctly foul smell or contains food with an unexpected appearance should be discarded. Do not taste it to decide.</p>
<p>Botulism is rare, but its seriousness is why official guidance treats suspicious containers conservatively. Dangerous contamination is not guaranteed to announce itself with an obvious smell or taste.</p>
<h2>Dented is not the same as bulging</h2>
<p>A <strong>dent</strong> is caused by force from outside pushing metal inward. A <strong>bulge</strong> is pressure from inside pushing the ends or walls outward. Bulging is a much clearer warning sign and should not be “fixed” by pressing the lid back down or cooling the can.</p>
<h2>Can cooking rescue food from a damaged can?</h2>
<p>It is not an appropriate strategy for a container whose seal may have failed. Commercial canning safety depends on the entire system—thermal process, closure and storage—not on trying to reprocess suspicious food in a home kitchen. If the package shows discard signs, discard it without tasting.</p>
<h2>What about rust?</h2>
<p>Light surface rust that wipes away is different from deep corrosion. If rust has caused pitting, weakness, holes or damage around the seams, discard the can. Long-term damp storage is also unsuitable for canned foods.</p>
<h2>Once opened, the rules change</h2>
<p>After opening, the food is no longer protected by its original hermetic system. Follow the producer's instructions, refrigerate when required and use within the recommended period. See our guide to <a href="/en/can-you-store-open-can-in-fridge-why-transfer-container/">storing opened canned food in the fridge</a>.</p>
<h2>A ten-second check</h2>
<p>Ask: <strong>Is the dent small? Is it away from seams? Is there no leakage? Is the can not swollen? Is there no deep rust? Is the metal neither folded nor punctured?</strong> If all are favourable, the damage may be cosmetic. If any serious warning sign is present, it is not worth the risk.</p>
